---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import SwatchGrid from '../components/SwatchGrid.astro';
import { SEASONS } from '../lib/seasons-data';
import { SEASON_PAGES } from '../lib/season-pages';

const summer = SEASONS.summer;
const winter = SEASONS.winter;

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Does grey hair change the colours that suit me?',
    answer:
      'It changes the contrast, not the undertone. Your skin leans the same way it always did, so the colour family that suited you still suits you. What changes is strength: hair that has lost its depth sits closer to your skin, and a shade that used to look striking can now look louder than the face it is next to.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I avoid grey clothing with grey hair?',
    answer:
      'Only the grey that matches your hair. A cool mid-grey jumper under silver hair blends the two together and flattens both. A charcoal or a soft blue-grey sits at a different depth and does the opposite, giving the hair something to stand against.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I still wear warm colours?',
    answer:
      'If your skin is warm, yes. Silver hair with warm skin is common and it is not a contradiction — take the softer warm shades, camel and warm beige over mustard and rust, so the warmth does not overpower hair that no longer has any depth to answer it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does black feel harder than it used to?',
    answer:
      'Black is the deepest thing you can put next to your face, and grey hair is one of the lightest. The gap between them is wider than it was, so black now casts a shadow that the hair cannot balance. Charcoal, navy and deep grey-blue do the same job with less of a drop.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the single easiest change to make?',
    answer:
      'Swap the shirt nearest your face. One soft white or powder blue top in place of a cream or mustard one changes more than a new coat, because that is where light reflects up into your skin.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Best Colours to Wear With Grey Hair | colormatch"
  description="The best colors to wear with gray hair, with names and hex codes. Why greying changes contrast rather than undertone, which shades to move towards, and the two mistakes that flatten silver hair."
  path="/best-colors-to-wear-with-gray-hair/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }]}
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell hero__inner stack">
      <h1>The best colours to wear with grey hair</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Going grey changes the contrast in your face, not the undertone underneath it. That one
        distinction explains almost everything about what suddenly works and what suddenly does not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <p>
          For most of your life your hair was the darkest thing in your face, and colours had to hold
          their own against it. Grey and silver hair is light and cool, which narrows the gap between
          your hair and your skin. The palette that suited you still suits you — your skin has not
          changed — but the strongest members of it now shout over a face that has less depth to answer
          them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things follow, and they are the whole of the advice. Soften the loudest shades by a
          notch, and stop using whatever colour your hair now is as a clothing colour near your face.
        </p>
      </div>

      <SwatchGrid
        title="Shades that suit most grey hair"
        description="Cool, gentle and clear enough to give the hair something to stand against. These are the summer palette's softest members, and they are where to start if you are not sure of your season."
        swatches={summer.wear.slice(0, 6)}
      />

      <SwatchGrid
        title="If your colouring is strong rather than soft"
        description="Some people keep a marked contrast between dark eyes, deep skin and white hair. That colouring can carry the winter palette at full strength, and softening it would be the mistake."
        swatches={winter.wear.slice(0, 6)}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <h2>The two mistakes</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Wearing your own hair colour</h3>
          <p>
            A grey jumper at the same depth as grey hair makes the two read as one continuous thing, and
            the face in the middle of it disappears. Keep clothing greys either clearly darker — charcoal,
            slate — or clearly lighter, and let the hair be the only silver in the picture.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Keeping the black you always wore</h3>
          <p>
            Black under white hair is the widest contrast available, and it tends to pull the shadows in
            your face downwards. Navy and charcoal read as smart in exactly the same way without the drop,
            which is why they are the usual swap once hair lightens.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <p>
          A third habit is worth naming, though it is less of a mistake than a missed opportunity: beige
          and other colours that sit close to skin tone. With less depth in the hair, a near-skin colour
          leaves nothing anywhere in the picture for the eye to settle on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <h2>Which palette is actually yours</h2>
        <p>
          Grey hair does not put everyone in the same group. Your undertone decides the palette, and
          greying only decides how strong a version of it to wear. The free colour report reads your skin
          rather than your hair, which is exactly what you want here — it takes about a minute and asks
          for nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/">Find your palette</a>
        </p>
        <ul class="tips">
          <li>
            <a href={`/${SEASON_PAGES.summer.slug}/`}>The summer palette</a> — where most silver-haired
            colourings are comfortable.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={`/${SEASON_PAGES.winter.slug}/`}>The winter palette</a> — for colouring that keeps a
            strong contrast.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/skin-tone-colors/">Find your undertone</a> — the part greying does not touch.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <Faq title="Questions about grey hair and colour" items={faq} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
